ted at  himself  it seemed based on the public  information that i had seen before  yesterday's indictment that he might  have been successful in doing so again  regarding the january 6th attack but as  we now know um the the department of  justice has plenty of information  regarding his involvement or his alleged  involvement i should say in planning for  the criminal activities that both  keepers engaged in on that day  we understand there are an estimated 38  000 members of oath keepers across the  u.s  these are what former military and  police  officers also like stewart rhodes  they're well educated some of them are  even ivy league graduates i mean this is  a different level of threat that we are  looking at  or or or is it not how would you  describe it  it's a little bit difficult to assess um  what we might think of as demographic  information about oath keepers  the group has long claimed more than 30  or 35 000 dues paying members the  watchdog organizations like the  anti-defamation league and the southern  poverty law center have estimated that  there's no more than a few thousand  active dues paying members at any given  moment it's also not clear how much of  the membership actually has experience  in military or law enforcement  certainly the group organizes around  this idea that it tries to recruit and  is primarily made up of people with such  service  but the group also welcomes people  without such service and it's just  really difficult for us to know  how many of its members have that  service how many of its members have  education and what types of education  that sort of granular information is  just not publicly available  we noticed that in the indictment it  reads that rhodes urged people to march  in mass to the nation's capital like the  people of serbia did 20 years ago can  you tell us anything about  that serbian connection here  as an organization oath keepers as well  as stewart roads individually are are  very fond of searching for historical  precedents for their understanding of  the world that they find themselves in  and for the crises that they perceive  more commonly they will point to the  american revolutionary war or the war  from independence from great britain to  find those historical precedents but  they have also pointed to as you say um  conflicts in the balkans they've pointed  to world war ii um and resistance to uh  the nazi regime to try to find  precedence for the activity that they're  uh  involved in or planning for or the  different events that they perceive i  don't think that necessarily means  there's any formal connection that the  group has  with participants in that balkan's  conflict though  um the oath keepers they often refer to  the founding fathers of the united  states  thomas jefferson for example he was an  advocate of generational renewal saying  that each generation must conduct its  own revolution  but why are we talking about this and  the oath keepers now in the 21st century  there's a long history in america of  um different  uh people with different political views  political identities um fighting over  the legacy of those founding fathers um  there's an excellent book um the  author's name is eluding me the book is  called the jefferson rule which talks  about how even the founders themselves  fought over the their own legacy in the  decades after the nation was founded so  this is nothing new to try to claim that  legacy and to try to  look to their writing to inform  contemporary politics oathkeepers has  found a particularly pernicious way to  do this in their indications of violence  and in their justifications for violence  but it's certainly not confined to them  in in your book you you also note that  militias like the oath keepers have  increased in numbers and in members  since 2008 that's when barack obama  became  u.s president that i'm wondering how  much of this is connected  to  to white supremacy and to the fears of  whites being replaced by minorities in  the united states is that driving this  surge in in membership  this is actually a really difficult  question that you're asking  um and and one that is the subject of  lively debate among researchers on this  field my own view is that  oath keepers as an organization is not  organized around perceived racial  identity  it is not an organization that is  motivated around perceived whiteness or  perceived threats to whiteness however  it is certainly the case  that people who are motivated by a  perceived racial identity can find uh  common cause with individuals uh within  the oath keepers organization and may  even join themselves the oath keepers  organization is organized around a  perception that government is tyrannical  and a desire to push back against that  perceived tyrannical government  this is something that racists have also  put forward  in other contexts in american history so  it's certainly familiar in that sense  but i think it is um overly simplistic  and uh  to to try to depict oath keepers as an  organization as part of a broader white  supremacist movement within the u.s yeah  it's fascinating on what you're saying  there and i wish we had more time to  talk about this  more in depth mr jackson but i'm sure  that we will have the opportunity to  talk again  sam jackson assistant presser professor  at the university of albany and author  of the book oath keepers patriotism and  the edge of violence in a right-wing  anti-government group professor jackson  we appreciate your time and your  insights tonight thank you  thank you
 

